[[:db/retract 17592186316573 :answer-field/choices 17592186316160] [:db/retract 17592186316824 :answer-field/choices 17592186315736] {:db/id 17592186166960 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3220fe7f-4991-5b98-91eb-d53f851e3473" "question-7b12d98f-f620-5c11-959f-ef743de24735" "question-359f2182-7c29-5b59-8d6e-b6e369490bd5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166575 :question/problem "![](images/e3/e34a1c2d8bdb840fb249000a5d13a87a5d8253c63e84e5478e8f3b50abb4fa35.svg)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166961 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a6f140ac-a7bd-5b1b-9932-ff6284a345e8" "question-2dec04ba-d895-5497-b51c-f40bad0126da" "question-45e143eb-b4b1-5a1c-8003-efa5644a6e01"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166576 :question/problem "![](images/52/52dd34274df338ede8449a4a66a101b3c54089a15c3b4f25d21ef1884a5af6ec.svg)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nUse this Fourier series to find the sum of the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3220fe7f-4991-5b98-91eb-d53f851e3473" :question/id #uuid "3220fe7f-4991-5b98-91eb-d53f851e3473" :question/math-academy-id "q-343384" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae24059b-d54d-5a3b-8f23-8c4f46c590bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae24059b-d54d-5a3b-8f23-8c4f46c590bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae24059b-d54d-5a3b-8f23-8c4f46c590bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b2c53509-fd31-5311-9043-5230d18ec8a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2c53509-fd31-5311-9043-5230d18ec8a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2c53509-fd31-5311-9043-5230d18ec8a2" :answer/id #uuid "b2c53509-fd31-5311-9043-5230d18ec8a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b12d98f-f620-5c11-959f-ef743de24735" :question/id #uuid "7b12d98f-f620-5c11-959f-ef743de24735" :question/math-academy-id "q-343386" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0] \\\\ {e}^{x}, & x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bd811455-ce81-5be1-8d33-3a112d11cc83"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd811455-ce81-5be1-8d33-3a112d11cc83" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd811455-ce81-5be1-8d33-3a112d11cc83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-54b97394-ead1-526b-8c32-cf56b7a9ff77" :answer-field/choices ["answer-54b97394-ead1-526b-8c32-cf56b7a9ff77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54b97394-ead1-526b-8c32-cf56b7a9ff77" :answer/id #uuid "54b97394-ead1-526b-8c32-cf56b7a9ff77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317396 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{3},0] \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-359f2182-7c29-5b59-8d6e-b6e369490bd5" :question/id #uuid "359f2182-7c29-5b59-8d6e-b6e369490bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343379" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ {x}^{2}-1, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-1.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-1)={e}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa784df0-a843-57dd-8cd1-3551bd0bd3ed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa784df0-a843-57dd-8cd1-3551bd0bd3ed" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa784df0-a843-57dd-8cd1-3551bd0bd3ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-09e9fd2c-76ea-5b24-b745-10e9e082024f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09e9fd2c-76ea-5b24-b745-10e9e082024f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09e9fd2c-76ea-5b24-b745-10e9e082024f" :answer/id #uuid "09e9fd2c-76ea-5b24-b745-10e9e082024f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375031 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-26, & x\\in [-5,0) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=5.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6f140ac-a7bd-5b1b-9932-ff6284a345e8" :question/id #uuid "a6f140ac-a7bd-5b1b-9932-ff6284a345e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343411" :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{\\pi (4{n}^{2}-1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{4{n}^{2}-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\pi  & =2-4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2-\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{4{n}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b09fcbfd-30a5-5ba2-885a-3f7d1ddc7d3d" "answer-field-cb4c04ac-867a-5bdf-a7eb-d4c641f208eb" "answer-field-fba6274c-d3f7-52f9-9f2c-91008e14aece" "answer-field-5dcd70b4-be7e-51eb-9cd3-d49e4071ecaa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b09fcbfd-30a5-5ba2-885a-3f7d1ddc7d3d" :answer-field/id #uuid "b09fcbfd-30a5-5ba2-885a-3f7d1ddc7d3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9b850642-cfa9-5fd6-9164-3b255159de5e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d01962eb-7bb1-5ea1-84f9-51cf3648df51" "answer-de2c2611-50be-5830-9c84-ab2f78498cbe" "answer-31f37a56-45d5-540a-8069-049c124751e7" "answer-9b850642-cfa9-5fd6-9164-3b255159de5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d01962eb-7bb1-5ea1-84f9-51cf3648df51" :answer/id #uuid "d01962eb-7bb1-5ea1-84f9-51cf3648df51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} 