e77429" :answer/id #uuid "11d097dd-7b04-5826-8407-30ac43e77429" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6e8c9e1-3c56-5e2c-999b-ca6e1bf63e65" :answer/id #uuid "a6e8c9e1-3c56-5e2c-999b-ca6e1bf63e65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167294 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186316573 :answer-field/correct 17592186315566 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317401 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186316824 :answer-field/correct 17592186315133 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-45e143eb-b4b1-5a1c-8003-efa5644a6e01" :question/id #uuid "45e143eb-b4b1-5a1c-8003-efa5644a6e01" :question/math-academy-id "q-343396" :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)={x}^{2}$ defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{4}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{16(-1)^{n}}{{\\pi }^{2}{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{16}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & =-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :questio