00-9ac7-1dbfaef906bf" "answer-7e56db31-f6d3-51d5-b7fe-086fdc013aee" "answer-5b9c7324-afe7-5ebc-8a5b-1a3b5a88673a" "answer-009c74af-e0c5-5eaa-a5d3-353f6408788f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b41eaed-2cee-5400-9ac7-1dbfaef906bf" :answer/id #uuid "3b41eaed-2cee-5400-9ac7-1dbfaef906bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e56db31-f6d3-51d5-b7fe-086fdc013aee" :answer/id #uuid "7e56db31-f6d3-51d5-b7fe-086fdc013aee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b9c7324-afe7-5ebc-8a5b-1a3b5a88673a" :answer/id #uuid "5b9c7324-afe7-5ebc-8a5b-1a3b5a88673a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-009c74af-e0c5-5eaa-a5d3-353f6408788f" :answer/id #uuid "009c74af-e0c5-5eaa-a5d3-353f6408788f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168046 :tutorial/content "A powerful application of Fourier series is *evaluating infinite sums*. The strategy is to find a known Fourier series that contains the sum we want, and then choose a convenient value of $x$ to plug in.\n\nFor example, consider the function $F(x)=x^2$ defined on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx).\n$$\n\nLet's use this to find the value of the infinite sum $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2}.$ \n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(n\\cdot0\\right) = \\cos\\left(0\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nBy the *pointwise convergence theorem*, since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(0\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, we have the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} = -\\dfrac{\\pi^2}{12}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166993 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
